# 6. GAME TWO — THE SCENE LAB

Working title: "The Scene Does Not Lie."

## Core concept

The learner sees an illustrated scene and receives an Arabic instruction.

The task is to select the scene or action that accurately represents the instruction.

Use interactive scenes, not only a list of static answer buttons.

Example:

"الْكِتَابُ تَحْتَ الطَّاوِلَةِ."

Show three clear alternatives:

- A book under a table.
- A book on a table.
- A book beside a table.

The learner selects the accurate scene.

## Progression

Start with:

- Objects and names.
- Colors.
- Simple location words.

Then introduce:

- Actions and verbs.
- Descriptive sentences.
- Pronouns.
- Short instructions.
- Multiple relationships within a single scene.

Eventually, require the player to select the correct components to construct a scene that matches an Arabic sentence.

The player might move objects into positions or ask a character to perform a specified action.

## Educational rules

Every visual scene must accurately represent the target sentence.

Do not include ambiguous images when assessing a specific linguistic relationship.

Where several interpretations are reasonable, accept the valid answers.

After an incorrect choice, explain the relevant language concept briefly.

Reuse the same verified vocabulary across multiple scenes and games.

