This is one of the most important architectural requirements.

All games must use a shared educational data model.

If a learner discovers the meaning of "أَمَام" in the Neighborhood, that knowledge must be available to other relevant games, lessons, and review activities.

Create reusable vocabulary records containing, as appropriate:

- Stable vocabulary ID.
- Arabic form with verified diacritics.
- Locale-specific explanations.
- Transliteration where educationally appropriate.
- Part of speech.
- Validated grammatical information.
- Approved audio reference.
- Source references.
- Learning-level metadata.
- Related forms and terms.
- Example sentences.
- Editorial review status.

Create lesson and game-task records that can reference the shared vocabulary.

A game must not independently create a different translation for a word already defined in the canonical content system.

Create a skill map covering areas such as:

- Word recognition.
- Vocabulary comprehension.
- Spatial language.
- Instructions.
- Listening comprehension.
- Sentence comprehension.
- Basic grammar.
- Morphology.
- Quranic vocabulary.

Use demonstrated learner responses to estimate progress.

Clearly distinguish exposure, lesson completion, and demonstrated mastery.

Do not give learners inflated mastery percentages simply because they opened a game.

