# 18. RESPONSIVE CONTROLS AND ACCESSIBILITY

The games must work on phones and desktops.

For mobile:

- Use large touch targets.
- Keep important controls within comfortable reach.
- Prevent accidental page scrolling while using movement controls, without trapping users unnecessarily.
- Scale the world to the viewport.
- Provide clear pause and exit controls.
- Avoid requiring precise mouse movements.
- Support portrait orientation first where reasonable, and handle landscape gracefully.

For desktop:

- Support keyboard controls and focus navigation.
- Include understandable on-screen control instructions.
- Prevent keyboard input from interfering with text fields or unrelated interface elements.

Accessibility:

- Use suitable text contrast.
- Provide non-color indicators of correctness.
- Include accessible labels for major actions.
- Support scalable text.
- Make critical game instructions available as text.
- Do not make audio the only means of understanding a task.
- Respect reduced-motion settings.
- Keep a practical way to pause, restart, or exit an activity.

Use clear language and avoid overwhelming beginners with simultaneous visual, audio, and written instructions.

